{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775347899-J0vDUzmiOOdZJFpgPIkC","role":"assistant","created":1775347903,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me try a different approach. I'll look for archived Federal Reserve data from the 1950s specifically. Looking at historical records, the pound was pegged at $2.80 following the 1949 devaluation through most of the early 1950s, until it was devalued again in 1967.\n\nFor the task at hand:\n- Feb 28, 1950 - uses 1949 exchange rates\n- Feb 28, 1951 - uses 1950 exchange rates  \n- Feb 29, 1952 - uses 1951 exchange rates\n\nThe devaluation in September 1949 changed the rate from $4.03 to $2.80 per pound. So the 1949 average would reflect this change. Let me search for more specific data to confirm.\n\nActually, I need to use monthly data for each year and compute arithmetic mean. Let me search for specific historical data.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_6f78d7f8a1a843429aff823d","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"curl -sL \"https://web.archive.org/web/19520101000000*/https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/h10/\" 2>&1 | head -40"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
